WEBVTT

00:00.230 --> 00:01.770
- [Instructor] So if you've
been watching these videos,

00:01.770 --> 00:03.490
you know that we have a lot of scenarios

00:03.490 --> 00:05.210
where people seem to be walking up to us

00:05.210 --> 00:07.619
on the street and asking
us to do math problems.

00:07.619 --> 00:09.696
And I guess this will be no different.

00:09.696 --> 00:11.670
So let's say someone walks up to you

00:11.670 --> 00:15.093
on the street and says, "Quick, you,

00:16.099 --> 00:21.099
"x squared plus five x
plus eight over x plus two,

00:22.177 --> 00:23.847
"what can this be simplified to?

00:23.847 --> 00:25.667
"Or what is x squared
plus five x plus eight

00:25.667 --> 00:27.320
"divided by x plus two?"

00:27.320 --> 00:30.630
Pause this video and see if
you can work through that.

00:30.630 --> 00:33.420
So there's two ways that
we can approach this.

00:33.420 --> 00:35.610
We can try to factor our numerator,

00:35.610 --> 00:37.440
and see if we have a common factor there,

00:37.440 --> 00:40.450
or we can try to use
algebraic long division.

00:40.450 --> 00:43.140
Let's first try to factor this numerator.

00:43.140 --> 00:45.170
And we would ideally want x plus two

00:45.170 --> 00:46.830
to be one of the factors.

00:46.830 --> 00:49.500
So let's see, what two
numbers can add up to five,

00:49.500 --> 00:51.520
and when I multiply 'em I get to eight,

00:51.520 --> 00:53.320
and ideally two is one of them.

00:53.320 --> 00:55.180
So I can think of two and three.

00:55.180 --> 01:00.180
But two times three is going
to be equal to six, not eight.

01:00.610 --> 01:02.630
And I can't think of anything else.

01:02.630 --> 01:05.470
But that still gives us some progress.

01:05.470 --> 01:07.340
Because, what if we did say,

01:07.340 --> 01:08.780
all right, let's rewrite part of it.

01:08.780 --> 01:12.180
What if we were to write
x squared plus five x,

01:12.180 --> 01:13.960
and we wanna write a plus six,

01:13.960 --> 01:17.770
because that actually would
be divisible by x plus two.

01:17.770 --> 01:19.900
So I'm gonna write a plus six.

01:19.900 --> 01:21.230
But of course we have an eight here,

01:21.230 --> 01:24.060
so then we're going to have
an extra two right over there.

01:24.060 --> 01:28.630
And then all of that is
divisible by x plus two.

01:28.630 --> 01:33.630
And now I can rewrite this
part, up here in orange.

01:34.020 --> 01:36.930
That is x plus two times x plus three.

01:36.930 --> 01:37.990
So let me write it here.

01:37.990 --> 01:42.310
x plus two times x plus three,

01:42.310 --> 01:44.430
I still have that plus
two sitting out there

01:44.430 --> 01:47.010
in the numerator, plus two.

01:47.010 --> 01:49.990
And then all of that over x plus two.

01:49.990 --> 01:54.260
Or I could write this as
being over x plus two.

01:54.260 --> 01:58.860
And this being over x plus two.

01:58.860 --> 02:00.120
All I did is I said, hey,

02:00.120 --> 02:02.029
if I have something plus something else

02:02.029 --> 02:04.610
over x plus two, that could
be the first something

02:04.610 --> 02:06.640
over x plus two plus the second something

02:06.640 --> 02:08.140
over x plus two.

02:08.140 --> 02:10.730
And then here, we can say, hey, look,

02:10.730 --> 02:14.880
this first part, as long as x
does not equal negative two,

02:14.880 --> 02:17.720
because then we would
be changing the domain,

02:17.720 --> 02:20.420
then these two would cancel out.

02:20.420 --> 02:21.690
What you could say, hey, I'm just dividing

02:21.690 --> 02:24.010
the numerator and the
denominator by x plus two.

02:24.010 --> 02:29.010
And so this would be equal
to x plus three, plus,

02:29.430 --> 02:31.980
and I don't necessarily even
have to put parentheses there,

02:31.980 --> 02:36.980
plus two over x plus two.

02:39.150 --> 02:43.290
And I would have to constrain the domain

02:43.290 --> 02:46.960
so this is for x does
not equal negative two.

02:46.960 --> 02:50.280
So in this situation, we had a remainder.

02:50.280 --> 02:54.160
And people will refer to
the two as the remainder.

02:54.160 --> 02:55.900
We divide it as far as we can,

02:55.900 --> 02:58.360
but it still remains to be done

02:58.360 --> 03:00.830
to divide the two by x plus two.

03:00.830 --> 03:05.740
And so we would refer to
the two as the remainder.

03:05.740 --> 03:08.235
Now that wasn't too difficult, but it also

03:08.235 --> 03:11.050
wasn't too straightforward and we'll see

03:11.050 --> 03:14.320
that this is a situation where
the algebraic long division

03:14.320 --> 03:16.300
is actually a little bit
more straightforward.

03:16.300 --> 03:17.750
So let's try that out.

03:17.750 --> 03:19.112
Once again, pause this
video and see if you can

03:19.112 --> 03:22.490
figure out what this is through
algebraic long division.

03:22.490 --> 03:26.830
So we're trying to take x
plus two and divide it into

03:28.040 --> 03:32.270
x squared plus five x plus eight.

03:32.270 --> 03:35.890
Look at the highest degree
terms, the x and the x squared.

03:35.890 --> 03:38.630
x goes into x squared x times,

03:38.630 --> 03:40.950
put it in the first degree column.

03:40.950 --> 03:45.840
x times two is two x,
x times x is x squared,

03:45.840 --> 03:49.860
subtract these from x squared plus five x

03:49.860 --> 03:53.100
and we get five x minus two x is three x.

03:53.100 --> 03:56.390
x squared minus x
squared, that's just zero.

03:56.390 --> 03:58.767
Bring down that eight.

03:58.767 --> 04:01.330
Look at the highest-degree term.

04:01.330 --> 04:04.740
And we get x goes into
three x three times.

04:04.740 --> 04:06.410
Put that in the constant column,

04:06.410 --> 04:09.960
or the zeroth degree
column, so plus three.

04:09.960 --> 04:14.560
Three times two is six,
three times x is three x.

04:14.560 --> 04:17.720
Subtract ds, and we are left with,

04:17.720 --> 04:19.970
let me scroll down a little bit,

04:19.970 --> 04:21.930
you're left with, those cancel out,

04:21.930 --> 04:24.340
and you're left with eight minus six,

04:24.340 --> 04:27.990
which is, indeed, equal to two.

04:27.990 --> 04:30.300
And we could say, hey, we
don't really know how to divide

04:30.300 --> 04:33.610
x plus two into two for an arbitrary x,

04:33.610 --> 04:36.160
so we will say, hey, this
is going to be equal to

04:36.160 --> 04:40.467
x plus three with a remainder of two.

04:43.890 --> 04:46.470
Now once again, if you wanted
to rewrite that original

04:46.470 --> 04:48.690
expression, and you wanted
it to be completely the same,

04:48.690 --> 04:51.860
including the domain, you
would have to constrain,

04:51.860 --> 04:53.670
you would have to constrain the domain,

04:53.670 --> 04:54.713
just like that.
